## 5. Leave Allocation Policies

### 5.1 Anniversary-Based Allocation

Allocation periods follow each employee's service anniversary, not the calendar year.

Example: If an employee's allocation period starts on 15 August, the next allocation period starts on the following 15 August.

When leave spans a period boundary, deductions are assigned to the appropriate period according to the dates of leave.

### 5.2 CL — Casual Leave

- Annual allocation: 12 days.
- First allocation: after completing one year of service.
- No allocation on the joining date.
- Carry-forward: maximum 5 unused days from the previous period.
- Only CL carries forward.
- Carried-forward days must not be counted twice.

### 5.3 SL — Sick Leave

- Annual allocation: 6 days.
- First allocation: after completing one year of service.
- No allocation on the joining date.
- No carry-forward.

### 5.4 LWP — Leave Without Pay

- First-year limit: 12 days.
- Subsequent limit: 12 days per year.
- LWP is unpaid and does not deduct from paid-leave balances.
- Count LWP usage only after approval.
- Enforce the applicable yearly limit during application validation and approval.
- If no leave is applied for, HR/admin determines attendance through the normal attendance process; the system does not automatically mark the employee absent.

### 5.5 WFH

- Grant 5 days annually after completing 6 years of service.
- The grant recurs each year after eligibility is reached.
- Unused WFH days do not carry forward.
- Each allocation has a fixed expiry date.
- A global rule change affects future allocations only.

### 5.6 Other Leave Types and Special Grants

MT, PT, OT, and PL use the configured eligibility/allocation rules applicable to them.

Administrators can grant additional leave for exceptional situations, such as accidents or emergencies. These grants must record the amount, grant source, administrator, and expiry date where applicable.

LWP remains unpaid; it is not a paid-leave balance.

